<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" standalone="yes"?><rss version="2.0" xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><channel><title>4. Resampling and regularisation on SPM 405 — Machine Learning</title><link>https://ndungundegwa.github.io/spm405-notes/lecture-04/</link><description>Recent content in 4. Resampling and regularisation on SPM 405 — Machine Learning</description><generator>Hugo</generator><language>en</language><atom:link href="https://ndungundegwa.github.io/spm405-notes/lecture-04/index.xml" rel="self" type="application/rss+xml"/><item><title>Opening</title><link>https://ndungundegwa.github.io/spm405-notes/lecture-04/opening/</link><pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://ndungundegwa.github.io/spm405-notes/lecture-04/opening/</guid><description>&lt;h1 id="opening"&gt;Opening&lt;a class="anchor" href="#opening"&gt;#&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h1&gt;&#10;&lt;h2 id="slide-2"&gt;Today&lt;a class="anchor" href="#slide-2"&gt;#&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&#10;&lt;blockquote class='book-hint '&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;Part 1: Resampling: the validation set; cross-validation; the bootstrap. Part 2: Regularisation: too many inputs; ridge regression; the lasso; choosing \(\lambda\) by cross-validation&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;/blockquote&gt;&lt;blockquote class='book-hint '&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;Reading: ISLP Chapter 5 (5.1–5.2) and Chapter 6 (6.1–6.2). ESL Sections 7.10–7.11 and 3.4&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;/blockquote&gt;&lt;p&gt;ESL 7.10 covers cross-validation (including the “wrong way” of slide &lt;a href="https://ndungundegwa.github.io/spm405-notes/lecture-04/resampling/#slide-10"&gt;10&lt;/a&gt;), ESL 7.11 the bootstrap, ESL 3.4 ridge and lasso.&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;blockquote class='book-hint '&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;Notebook: lecture04_resampling_regularisation.ipynb on Google Classroom&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;/blockquote&gt;&lt;h2 id="slide-3"&gt;From last week: your first models&lt;a class="anchor" href="#slide-3"&gt;#&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&#10;&lt;blockquote class='book-hint '&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;Each group fitted a first linear or logistic regression&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>Resampling</title><link>https://ndungundegwa.github.io/spm405-notes/lecture-04/resampling/</link><pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://ndungundegwa.github.io/spm405-notes/lecture-04/resampling/</guid><description>&lt;h1 id="resampling"&gt;Resampling&lt;a class="anchor" href="#resampling"&gt;#&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h1&gt;&#10;&lt;h2 id="slide-5"&gt;Example: calibrating a thermistor&lt;a class="anchor" href="#slide-5"&gt;#&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&#10;&lt;figure class="photo"&gt;&#10; &lt;img src="https://ndungundegwa.github.io/spm405-notes/photos/thermistor.jpg" alt="An NTC thermistor: its resistance falls with temperature along a curve, not a line." width="460" loading="lazy" /&gt;&#10; &lt;figcaption&gt;An NTC thermistor: its resistance falls with temperature along a curve, not a line.&#10; &lt;span class="credit"&gt;Photo: Soumyapatra13, &lt;a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:NTC_Thermistor.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener"&gt;Wikimedia Commons&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0" target="_blank" rel="noopener"&gt;CC BY-SA 4.0&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&#10; &lt;/figcaption&gt;&#10;&lt;/figure&gt;&#10;&#10;&lt;figure class="slide-fig"&gt;&lt;img src="https://ndungundegwa.github.io/spm405-notes/fig/lecture-04/l04-4e3a3aeb2554.svg" width="346" alt="Figure: Example: calibrating a thermistor" loading="lazy" /&gt;&lt;/figure&gt;&#10;&lt;blockquote class='book-hint '&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;An NTC thermistor (negative temperature coefficient) is a resistor whose resistance \(R\) falls steeply as it warms&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;/blockquote&gt;&lt;p&gt;NTC means negative temperature coefficient. Thermistors are cheap, sensitive temperature sensors used in appliances, cars and&#10;instruments.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>Regularisation</title><link>https://ndungundegwa.github.io/spm405-notes/lecture-04/regularisation/</link><pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://ndungundegwa.github.io/spm405-notes/lecture-04/regularisation/</guid><description>&lt;h1 id="regularisation"&gt;Regularisation&lt;a class="anchor" href="#regularisation"&gt;#&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h1&gt;&#10;&lt;h2 id="slide-13"&gt;Too many inputs: an electronic nose&lt;a class="anchor" href="#slide-13"&gt;#&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&#10;&lt;figure class="photo"&gt;&#10; &lt;img src="https://ndungundegwa.github.io/spm405-notes/photos/gas_sensor.jpg" alt="The principle of a sensor array: each sensor reacts a little differently, giving a pattern." width="560" loading="lazy" /&gt;&#10; &lt;figcaption&gt;The principle of a sensor array: each sensor reacts a little differently, giving a pattern.&#10; &lt;span class="credit"&gt;Photo: Nanomaterials21, &lt;a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Colorimetric_Sensor_Array_Principles.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener"&gt;Wikimedia Commons&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0" target="_blank" rel="noopener"&gt;CC BY-SA 4.0&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&#10; &lt;/figcaption&gt;&#10;&lt;/figure&gt;&#10;&#10;&lt;figure class="diagram"&gt;&#10; &lt;img src="https://ndungundegwa.github.io/spm405-notes/diagrams/electronic_nose.svg" alt="An electronic nose: eight gas sensors give an 8-number smell pattern for each milk sample." width="760" loading="lazy" /&gt;&#10; &lt;figcaption&gt;An electronic nose: eight gas sensors give an 8-number smell pattern for each milk sample.&lt;/figcaption&gt;&#10;&lt;/figure&gt;&#10;&lt;blockquote class='book-hint '&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;A gas-sensor array (“electronic nose”) smells a milk sample with 40 channels&lt;/p&gt;</description></item></channel></rss>